00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:39.150
So let's start with
why we actually

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:41.150
are even having this
conversation to begin with.

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:46.130
So GKE On-Prem is obviously
a on-prem version of GKE.

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:48.810
Who uses GKE today?

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:49.310
Excellent.

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:52.310
So we got a lot about Kubernetes
folks here that understand it.

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:56.640
GKE On-Prem premise the idea
of bringing that to a platform

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.320
other than Google.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:01.220
And so that's because although
many people have mandated

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:04.370
public clouds-- they
want to use them, 73%--

00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:07.940
but only 10% of workloads
have actually moved over.

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:10.850
So there's still almost 90% of
workloads in many enterprises

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:12.540
that are still in
on-prem environments.

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:14.720
So how do you
unlock the potential

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:16.730
of using the latest
cloud technologies

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:19.410
but in those on-prem
environments?

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:22.672
And so some have even moved
data back in some cases.

00:02:22.672 --> 00:02:24.380
So we actually have
to contend with this.

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:26.750
There's always going to
be some element of hybrid

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:27.583
in most enterprises.

00:02:27.583 --> 00:02:30.125
They're not just going to pick
everything up and just move it

00:02:30.125 --> 00:02:31.300
all to the cloud tomorrow.

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:33.800
And even if they do, it's going
to be a transitional period.

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:35.930
So hybrid is really
pragmatic and persistent.

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:37.670
And so GKE On-Prem
is an opportunity

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:39.530
to bring some of
that cloud technology

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:44.310
into your existing
on-premises infrastructure.

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:46.000
Now, that shows up
in a lot of ways.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:48.500
There's a lot of reasons why
you might have to even continue

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:50.550
on that path over time.

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:52.700
So for example,
let's say you really

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:55.460
want to unlock the cloud
for your development teams.

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:56.450
You want CI/CD.

00:02:56.450 --> 00:02:58.370
You want full
automation pipelines.

00:02:58.370 --> 00:02:59.735
You want to use the latest
tools and technologies.

00:02:59.735 --> 00:03:01.735
You want to give them dev
environments that they

00:03:01.735 --> 00:03:03.290
can turn up with a snap, right?

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:05.610
But it turns out when you
go to run it in production,

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:09.020
it's on a ship, or it's in a
store, or it's in a factory.

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:10.610
You actually have
this split model of,

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:13.070
I need to be able to
build locally in the cloud

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:15.180
and then I need to
push to someplace else.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:17.850
So GKE On-Prem is giving
containers everywhere.

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:19.230
That's that model.

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:21.440
Another model that requires
hybrid and requires

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:25.790
this kind of mixed
environment is jurisdictional.

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:27.380
I'm writing code,
but fundamentally,

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:29.150
the actual execution,
or even the data

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:31.760
itself, has to live in
a specific location.

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:33.770
There's rules about
where it can reside.

00:03:33.770 --> 00:03:36.020
And therefore, I need
the cloud technologies

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:38.270
close to my data, which has
to stay in a certain spot,

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:41.360
where maybe there isn't
a cloud data center.

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:42.920
So that's another common model.

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:44.628
And then the third
we've seen quite often

00:03:44.628 --> 00:03:46.280
is even this
best-of-breed services.

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:48.470
I want to run some things
local against my data sets

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:49.053
through there.

00:03:49.053 --> 00:03:51.920
But then I also want to tap
into, say, machine learning

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:54.680
or data warehousing technology.

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:57.230
So I want to bridge these
architectures, where

00:03:57.230 --> 00:03:59.030
I have a local execution,
then I actually

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:03.200
tap into a data set in the
cloud or a certain function

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:04.010
in the cloud.

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:06.705
And so all of these are reasons
why you might actually end up

00:04:06.705 --> 00:04:09.080
having a hybrid scenario and
why you might need something

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:11.960
like GKE On-Prem.

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:14.890
In swoops Anthos, once called
Cloud Services Platform,

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:16.300
now called Anthos.

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:19.950
And the idea here is it's a
collection of cloud services,

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:22.370
but it's also a collection
of marketplace components.

00:04:22.370 --> 00:04:23.630
And we'll see some of that.

00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:26.270
You can actually tie-in to
the existing GCP marketplace

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:29.870
both for in the cloud but also
in the on-prem environment.

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:32.120
As well as the control
plane, the idea

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:35.010
being that once you've
taken this leap into using

00:04:35.010 --> 00:04:37.190
this hybrid model
that goes everywhere,

00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:38.480
you can now run that--

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:39.950
The same way you'd
run it on GCP,

00:04:39.950 --> 00:04:42.020
you run it on-prem
with the same tools

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:45.410
and technologies, the same back
end services and other clouds.

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:47.610
So that is the long
term goal here.

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:51.080
So we'll talk about how it
looks today a little bit more,

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:53.780
so the fundamentals
of that platform.

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:56.168
But really, the reason why
we think this makes sense

00:04:56.168 --> 00:04:58.460
and what we've been hearing
from a lot of our customers

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:01.340
about why this is
something they want one,

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:03.350
it's this ability to
modernize in place.

00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:06.860
I want to leverage the latest
in development technologies,

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:11.270
such as containers or Kubernetes
or even things like Istio

00:05:11.270 --> 00:05:12.710
for service mesh
management, but I

00:05:12.710 --> 00:05:15.530
want to do it where I have
my infrastructure today.

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:17.690
I want to learn how to
modernize what I have,

00:05:17.690 --> 00:05:20.750
as opposed to having to move
everything to the cloud first.

00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:23.510
| second reason why people
are liking this approach

00:05:23.510 --> 00:05:26.840
fundamentally is the ability
to automate policy and security

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:27.660
at scale.

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:29.870
So some of these components
in this platform--

00:05:29.870 --> 00:05:31.850
such as, say, Istio
for service mesh

00:05:31.850 --> 00:05:34.850
for managing your access
between individual components

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:37.880
or elements in your
container architecture

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:40.460
or config management, which
allows you to automatically

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:42.890
apply configuration
to your clusters--

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:45.740
are about the ability
to manage that sprawl

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:47.150
that you see that
starts to occur

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:49.400
when you have microservices
and containers in lots

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:50.490
of different places.

00:05:50.490 --> 00:05:53.092
How do I keep a consistent
policy on top of that?

00:05:53.092 --> 00:05:55.550
How do we have a consistent
security profile and a security

00:05:55.550 --> 00:05:58.470
stance and configuration
stance everywhere?

00:05:58.470 --> 00:06:00.500
And that's another
goal of what the Anthos

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:02.160
platform is really focused on.

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:04.520
And the last is really
that consistent experience,

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:07.250
not really so much for your
users, but for your operators,

00:06:07.250 --> 00:06:09.460
for your developers, and
for your administrators,

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:12.290
so they have a consistent set
of tools, a consistent approach

00:06:12.290 --> 00:06:13.550
that they take everywhere.

00:06:13.550 --> 00:06:17.060
So they're learning different
technologies, different tools

00:06:17.060 --> 00:06:19.750
on each cloud or
platform they use.

00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:23.040
They'll only need one, set one
tool chain across the board.

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:25.670
So they're writing code the same
way no matter where it runs.

00:06:25.670 --> 00:06:28.100
They're running the same kinds
of scripts for automation

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:29.930
no matter where it's
actually functioning.

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:32.480
And then ultimately, you
have the same dashboard

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:34.683
of visibility of what's
happening everywhere,

00:06:34.683 --> 00:06:37.100
no matter where that code is
running, no matter where it's

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:39.116
being developed or deployed.

00:06:39.116 --> 00:06:44.050
So that's fundamentally the
goal of the overall platform.

00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:46.845
And this is sort of
a little view into it

00:06:46.845 --> 00:06:49.220
to give you a sense of all
the pieces that play in there.

00:06:49.220 --> 00:06:51.048
And we're going to
talk more about--

00:06:51.048 --> 00:06:53.090
The GKE On-Prem is in that
bottom right, you see.

00:06:53.090 --> 00:06:57.740
That's where we've deployed
GKE to a specific cluster.

00:06:57.740 --> 00:07:00.167
But there's more to it when
you look at the full platform.

00:07:00.167 --> 00:07:02.000
In terms of things like
additional services,

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:04.352
you might tap into, say,
Stackdriver or Cloud Run,

00:07:04.352 --> 00:07:05.810
which was talked
about this morning

00:07:05.810 --> 00:07:08.870
in the keynote, binary
authorization for the ability

00:07:08.870 --> 00:07:11.480
to sign and certify
container images

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:14.090
and code that you're deploying.

00:07:14.090 --> 00:07:16.070
All of those things
are additional services

00:07:16.070 --> 00:07:17.587
you might add on
to your deployment,

00:07:17.587 --> 00:07:18.920
if you feel they're appropriate.

00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:21.075
Those can run both as
sort of a split model,

00:07:21.075 --> 00:07:22.700
a little bit in the
cloud, a little bit

00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:23.948
in your on-prem environment.

00:07:23.948 --> 00:07:25.740
And then there's this
Marketplace component

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:26.323
I mentioned.

00:07:26.323 --> 00:07:28.490
You saw actually probably
in the first day's keynote

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:30.170
there was a moment
where they showed

00:07:30.170 --> 00:07:33.570
just a dropdown selecting, say,
CloudBees and hitting a button

00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:34.070
and go.

00:07:34.070 --> 00:07:36.170
And that deploys
it to your cluster.

00:07:36.170 --> 00:07:38.480
The idea is that the GCP
Marketplace that's there

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:42.050
is tied directly in, so you can
deploy even purchased software,

00:07:42.050 --> 00:07:44.870
paid components directly to
your clusters, whether they

00:07:44.870 --> 00:07:46.442
be on-prem or in the cloud.

00:07:46.442 --> 00:07:48.650
And then below that, we have
that control plane layer

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:51.200
we're going to talk
a little bit about.

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:54.040
There's three big pieces in
there-- config management,

00:07:54.040 --> 00:07:57.050
the connect layer, and then, of
course, the service mesh, which

00:07:57.050 --> 00:08:01.810
is akin to Istio and sort of
a managed service mesh layer.

00:08:01.810 --> 00:08:05.565
GKE On-Prem is that bottom
right box, right there.

00:08:05.565 --> 00:08:07.940
And it's really about bringing
the cloud style automation

00:08:07.940 --> 00:08:09.890
to an on-premises model.

00:08:09.890 --> 00:08:12.440
So big elements of why
this makes a lot of sense

00:08:12.440 --> 00:08:14.727
is instead of
patching, upgrading,

00:08:14.727 --> 00:08:17.060
and troubleshooting your
cluster, you can simply rebuild

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:17.990
and deploy.

00:08:17.990 --> 00:08:20.990
It's much more automated
in terms of control,

00:08:20.990 --> 00:08:22.637
in terms of getting
that rolled out.

00:08:22.637 --> 00:08:24.220
And you can
programmatically provision

00:08:24.220 --> 00:08:25.710
and scale that infrastructure.

00:08:25.710 --> 00:08:30.080
So because now you've got tied
into the GKE On-Prem interface,

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:32.030
which looks very much
like the kubectl--

00:08:32.030 --> 00:08:34.909
there's a new thing
called gkectl--

00:08:34.909 --> 00:08:36.860
and it will go ahead
and spin up new clusters

00:08:36.860 --> 00:08:39.409
and actually upgraded versions
of your Kubernetes running

00:08:39.409 --> 00:08:40.733
on top of it for you.

00:08:40.733 --> 00:08:42.650
So it does the automation
of that provisioning

00:08:42.650 --> 00:08:45.080
of infrastructure
as part of that.

00:08:45.080 --> 00:08:47.240
And it also will upgrade
software via controllers,

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:48.012
not manually.

00:08:48.012 --> 00:08:49.970
So now, you actually are
getting the deployment

00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:52.280
of the latest versions
of Kubernetes,

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:56.060
the exact same versions
that are running on GKE

00:08:56.060 --> 00:08:57.360
in your on-prem environment.

00:08:57.360 --> 00:08:59.390
So there's no worries
about version mismatch

00:08:59.390 --> 00:09:02.780
across your platform.

00:09:02.780 --> 00:09:05.530
And so the comparisons, if
you look at it, GKE versus GKE

00:09:05.530 --> 00:09:08.180
On-Prem, I think I'll
skip the GKE side,

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:09.950
because sounds
like a lot of folks

00:09:09.950 --> 00:09:11.330
kind of know what's out there.

00:09:11.330 --> 00:09:13.340
But big notes to
make are that GKE

00:09:13.340 --> 00:09:16.310
has been in GA since
August of 2015,

00:09:16.310 --> 00:09:20.690
so longstanding
consumption usage pattern.

00:09:20.690 --> 00:09:23.300
It's a fully managed
Kubernetes control plane,

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:25.430
takes care of a lot of
those things for you.

00:09:25.430 --> 00:09:26.930
Now, on the GKE
On-Prem side, you'll

00:09:26.930 --> 00:09:29.620
see now you're getting turnkey,
production-grade conformant

00:09:29.620 --> 00:09:31.890
Kubernetes with
best-practice configuration.

00:09:31.890 --> 00:09:35.450
So what that means is just like
with GKE where we're giving you

00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:37.130
Kubernetes versions
to choose from that

00:09:37.130 --> 00:09:39.350
have been vetted and
verified, same thing

00:09:39.350 --> 00:09:40.460
happens with GKE On-Prem.

00:09:40.460 --> 00:09:43.010
You're using the same versions
of the open source Kubernetes

00:09:43.010 --> 00:09:45.890
platform that runs
in both environments.

00:09:45.890 --> 00:09:47.610
There's an easy
upgrade path that

00:09:47.610 --> 00:09:50.030
has been validated and tested
releases of Kubernetes.

00:09:50.030 --> 00:09:52.155
So you know you're picking
something that's already

00:09:52.155 --> 00:09:54.682
been tested and verified.

00:09:54.682 --> 00:09:56.390
And of course, you
will still have access

00:09:56.390 --> 00:09:58.940
to all those container services
that you use in the cloud.

00:09:58.940 --> 00:10:01.130
So you can still use
Container Registry.

00:10:01.130 --> 00:10:05.240
You can still use Cloud
Build for automation,

00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:07.570
Audit Logging, pieces
like that, as well as

00:10:07.570 --> 00:10:09.410
Stackdriver and other services.

00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:10.898
However, you can
also use your own.

00:10:10.898 --> 00:10:12.440
So there's no reason
why you couldn't

00:10:12.440 --> 00:10:15.530
set up your own private
Container Registry on-prem

00:10:15.530 --> 00:10:18.283
and point to that
instead of GCR.

00:10:18.283 --> 00:10:19.700
There's no reason
why you couldn't

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:23.030
have your own build pipelines
that you run locally

00:10:23.030 --> 00:10:26.080
and then deploy through those
in your on-prem environment.

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:27.740
So you're not tied
to those services,

00:10:27.740 --> 00:10:28.948
but they're available to you.

00:10:28.948 --> 00:10:30.770
So you have
flexibility and choice.

00:10:30.770 --> 00:10:33.200
I think choice is the word
we used a lot yesterday

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:34.130
to describe Anthos.

00:10:34.130 --> 00:10:36.963
Choice is what it's really
about, giving you options.

00:10:36.963 --> 00:10:38.630
And of course, you
have full integration

00:10:38.630 --> 00:10:41.390
with various other
open source components,

00:10:41.390 --> 00:10:45.080
like, say, Istio, also
managed through cloud service

00:10:45.080 --> 00:10:47.930
mesh, which is part of
the Anthos umbrella;

00:10:47.930 --> 00:10:50.480
Knative, which is then
also talked to you

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:51.690
about is Cloud Run.

00:10:51.690 --> 00:10:54.140
So you're seeing this
parallel, where the open source

00:10:54.140 --> 00:10:57.020
projects are also then
available as offerings

00:10:57.020 --> 00:10:58.340
through the Anthos platform.

00:10:58.340 --> 00:11:01.530
And then the Marketplace
solutions as well.

00:11:01.530 --> 00:11:04.670
So in terms of consistency,
the GKE versus GKE On-Prem,

00:11:04.670 --> 00:11:07.160
you see there's a lot
of similarities here.

00:11:07.160 --> 00:11:09.320
Operating systems
On-Prem is Ubuntu

00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:12.200
versus the
Container-Optimized OS.

00:11:12.200 --> 00:11:14.000
There's some minor
tool differences.

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:16.720
The console is the same. kubectl
is the same, or "kube ctl"

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:19.510
depending on what way you
like to pronounce that.

00:11:19.510 --> 00:11:24.410
glcoud swaps out for
gkectl, so "gke cuddle",

00:11:24.410 --> 00:11:26.370
which allows you to
spin up a new cluster.

00:11:26.370 --> 00:11:28.040
And that's how you
deploy user clusters

00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:29.510
inside the environment.

00:11:29.510 --> 00:11:30.950
Calico is consistent across.

00:11:30.950 --> 00:11:32.540
You've got Stackdriver.

00:11:32.540 --> 00:11:36.470
You've got EFK in GKE
On-Prem, but you can also

00:11:36.470 --> 00:11:37.820
use Stackdriver Logging.

00:11:37.820 --> 00:11:40.070
You can use whatever
logging tools you want.

00:11:40.070 --> 00:11:42.170
For monitoring,
Prometheus and Grafana

00:11:42.170 --> 00:11:44.918
are deployed automatically, but
you can add Stackdriver plugins

00:11:44.918 --> 00:11:45.960
as well if you want that.

00:11:45.960 --> 00:11:48.293
So you have the ability to
keep it separate and isolated

00:11:48.293 --> 00:11:51.320
or to actually bridge it in
with the existing services

00:11:51.320 --> 00:11:54.520
that GKE uses as well.

00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:57.140
What it kind of looks like--
and this is what we call

00:11:57.140 --> 00:12:01.250
the Kubeception model,
clusters within clusters--

00:12:01.250 --> 00:12:03.805
when you were to
deploy GKE On-Prem,

00:12:03.805 --> 00:12:05.180
fundamentally you
build out first

00:12:05.180 --> 00:12:06.263
an administrative cluster.

00:12:06.263 --> 00:12:07.310
That's that top box.

00:12:07.310 --> 00:12:11.150
That admin cluster has
its virtual machines

00:12:11.150 --> 00:12:14.600
that are the control plane,
and then actually, if you will,

00:12:14.600 --> 00:12:19.230
"masters" in sort of
the air quoted sense.

00:12:19.230 --> 00:12:20.810
They are actually
control plane nodes

00:12:20.810 --> 00:12:23.117
for the user clusters,
that then have elements

00:12:23.117 --> 00:12:24.200
that fall underneath that.

00:12:24.200 --> 00:12:27.560
So there's an admin cluster
that creates other clusters is

00:12:27.560 --> 00:12:29.400
the way to think about that.

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:32.600
So when you go ahead and run
gkectl to, say, spin up a user

00:12:32.600 --> 00:12:35.000
cluster, it will talk
to that admin control

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:39.560
plane, which works directly
with in V1 the vCenter APIs.

00:12:39.560 --> 00:12:41.960
So it works directly with
the VMware, vCenter APIs,

00:12:41.960 --> 00:12:45.170
to automatically
provision new VMs,

00:12:45.170 --> 00:12:48.990
as well as working directly
with, in this case, F5 BIG-IP,

00:12:48.990 --> 00:12:52.460
to provision network
address space and register

00:12:52.460 --> 00:12:54.763
addresses for external
access to the cluster.

00:12:54.763 --> 00:12:56.180
We'll cover some
of the networking

00:12:56.180 --> 00:12:57.990
components in a second.

00:12:57.990 --> 00:13:01.222
But fundamentally, that is
a fully automated deployment

00:13:01.222 --> 00:13:02.180
of a whole new cluster.

00:13:02.180 --> 00:13:03.550
Those images are spin up.

00:13:03.550 --> 00:13:05.360
They have Kubernetes
pre-installed in them.

00:13:05.360 --> 00:13:06.420
They come online.

00:13:06.420 --> 00:13:07.830
They join the cluster.

00:13:07.830 --> 00:13:11.510
And you can actually control
the sizing of those VMs

00:13:11.510 --> 00:13:12.560
when you deploy them.

00:13:12.560 --> 00:13:15.290
So it's all baked
in a configuration

00:13:15.290 --> 00:13:17.240
that you can run
once to do a dry run

00:13:17.240 --> 00:13:19.923
and then go ahead and
then deploy automatically.

00:13:19.923 --> 00:13:21.840
So then you can create
multiple user clusters.

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:24.050
So if today, you might be
using different clusters

00:13:24.050 --> 00:13:26.017
for different teams
or different services.

00:13:26.017 --> 00:13:27.350
You'd do the same thing On-Prem.

00:13:27.350 --> 00:13:29.000
You spin up a user
cluster for team

00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:31.600
x, a user cluster for team y.

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:33.920
And then they have
completely isolated clusters

00:13:33.920 --> 00:13:38.610
that you can control
individually.

00:13:38.610 --> 00:13:40.950
And then you can use
DHCP or static IP

00:13:40.950 --> 00:13:44.437
controls to decide how you're
going to allocate addresses.

00:13:44.437 --> 00:13:46.020
So you have a little
bit control there

00:13:46.020 --> 00:13:47.430
from a networking perspective.

00:13:47.430 --> 00:13:49.555
And networking is one area
where this is different.

00:13:49.555 --> 00:13:53.430
And we'll cover that in
just a second, because we

00:13:53.430 --> 00:13:55.440
have this diagram,
which I'm sure

00:13:55.440 --> 00:13:57.010
everyone's going
to want a photo of.

00:13:57.010 --> 00:13:58.968
Don't worry, it's going
to be in the video too.

00:13:58.968 --> 00:14:01.530
But the idea here
is that networking

00:14:01.530 --> 00:14:04.860
is one thing that's quite
different with GKE On-Prem.

00:14:04.860 --> 00:14:07.660
So when you think about
GKE running on the cloud,

00:14:07.660 --> 00:14:09.210
you also have-- it
has full control

00:14:09.210 --> 00:14:11.760
to the network infrastructure
upon which it runs.

00:14:11.760 --> 00:14:14.670
That means it's allowed
to set up load balancers,

00:14:14.670 --> 00:14:17.560
and make sure the routing
between IP ranges is set up,

00:14:17.560 --> 00:14:20.902
and the IP address rules
or the IP filtering rules,

00:14:20.902 --> 00:14:22.860
firewall rules are all
configured automatically

00:14:22.860 --> 00:14:23.730
for you.

00:14:23.730 --> 00:14:27.660
When you look at this running
in your on-prem environment,

00:14:27.660 --> 00:14:29.720
inside the cluster
things work great.

00:14:29.720 --> 00:14:32.130
All the components inside the
cluster can see each other.

00:14:32.130 --> 00:14:33.755
But then things coming
from the outside

00:14:33.755 --> 00:14:36.330
have to access those
services inside.

00:14:36.330 --> 00:14:38.410
So that's where there
is a subtle difference.

00:14:38.410 --> 00:14:41.510
And that's why there's a--

00:14:41.510 --> 00:14:44.567
In my example, in this
slide, it's using F5.

00:14:44.567 --> 00:14:46.650
Longer term, there's a
goal to bring your own load

00:14:46.650 --> 00:14:49.630
balancer that you might
choose as opposed to that.

00:14:49.630 --> 00:14:51.900
So this will be a
model where what

00:14:51.900 --> 00:14:55.080
happens is when you're
spinning up a cluster,

00:14:55.080 --> 00:14:58.350
it creates the node IPs, pod
IPs, and service IP addresses,

00:14:58.350 --> 00:14:59.880
are all automatically
created based

00:14:59.880 --> 00:15:01.050
on the ranges you give it.

00:15:01.050 --> 00:15:04.050
You allocate the 10 dot
range or whatever private IP

00:15:04.050 --> 00:15:05.680
range you want.

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:08.820
And then it will also, as
part of that spin-up process,

00:15:08.820 --> 00:15:12.540
allocate the ingress VIP, the
service VIP, and the control

00:15:12.540 --> 00:15:14.050
plane VIPs in F5.

00:15:14.050 --> 00:15:16.490
So now, all of those services
that you might deploy,

00:15:16.490 --> 00:15:18.240
or all of the components
you might deploy,

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:20.260
are accessible from
outside the cluster.

00:15:20.260 --> 00:15:22.320
So other components
in your network,

00:15:22.320 --> 00:15:26.192
inside your on-prem data
center, can now see those.

00:15:26.192 --> 00:15:27.900
You choose what you
expose, just like you

00:15:27.900 --> 00:15:31.080
choose what you expose in
GKE, by exposing a service

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:32.730
and making it available.

00:15:32.730 --> 00:15:35.860
Same concepts, you're still
exposing a load balancer,

00:15:35.860 --> 00:15:40.110
but you're choosing a particular
IP range that's known by F5,

00:15:40.110 --> 00:15:41.910
so it can actually
expose that out

00:15:41.910 --> 00:15:44.583
to the rest of the data center.

00:15:44.583 --> 00:15:46.750
We'll actually take a look
at that in just a second.

00:15:49.112 --> 00:15:51.570
The other thing that comes of
this is because that's great,

00:15:51.570 --> 00:15:54.053
now you've got Kubernetes
running in your data center,

00:15:54.053 --> 00:15:56.220
but you probably still want
to see what's happening,

00:15:56.220 --> 00:15:57.690
you still want to have
that kind of visibility.

00:15:57.690 --> 00:16:00.040
And so there is a
component that is optional.

00:16:00.040 --> 00:16:01.530
You choose to add it.

00:16:01.530 --> 00:16:03.870
It's called GKE
Connect, is a hub.

00:16:03.870 --> 00:16:07.190
It is essentially something
you deploy into that cluster.

00:16:07.190 --> 00:16:11.580
And what it does is it registers
that cluster with the GCP

00:16:11.580 --> 00:16:12.340
platform.

00:16:12.340 --> 00:16:14.340
So now, that cluster
shows up in your console

00:16:14.340 --> 00:16:16.690
just like any other
cluster you have running.

00:16:16.690 --> 00:16:20.210
So now you can see, oh, here's
my GKE cluster in us-central1-a

00:16:20.210 --> 00:16:23.280
and here's my GKE cluster
running in On-Prem,

00:16:23.280 --> 00:16:25.260
right next to each
other, dive right in.

00:16:25.260 --> 00:16:28.500
Now, the way that works
is it will actually

00:16:28.500 --> 00:16:32.520
install an agent inside
the GKE On-Prem cluster.

00:16:32.520 --> 00:16:36.180
And then that agent
will actually dial back

00:16:36.180 --> 00:16:38.250
using a gRPC TLS connection.

00:16:38.250 --> 00:16:39.990
So it's encrypted dial-back.

00:16:39.990 --> 00:16:41.490
You actually embed
secrets in there.

00:16:41.490 --> 00:16:42.930
So it's safe.

00:16:42.930 --> 00:16:45.390
It's part of the
manifest that is given

00:16:45.390 --> 00:16:47.580
to you during the registration.

00:16:47.580 --> 00:16:49.800
What happens is it will
actually callback to GCP

00:16:49.800 --> 00:16:51.930
and then announce, hey,
here's how I'm doing.

00:16:51.930 --> 00:16:52.963
Here's my alerts.

00:16:52.963 --> 00:16:54.630
Here's what's going
on with my services.

00:16:54.630 --> 00:16:57.870
Here's the info on my nodes
and my pods and storage.

00:16:57.870 --> 00:16:59.720
All that kind of stuff
is reported back up.

00:16:59.720 --> 00:17:02.220
So you can see it right there
in the console if you want to.

00:17:02.220 --> 00:17:05.460
It also gives you the ability
to feed deployment information

00:17:05.460 --> 00:17:06.339
back down.

00:17:06.339 --> 00:17:08.700
So from the console,
you can deploy things

00:17:08.700 --> 00:17:11.579
directly into an On-Prem
cluster, another thing

00:17:11.579 --> 00:17:13.790
that we're actually
going to take a look at.

00:17:13.790 --> 00:17:15.665
In fact, let's take a
look at that right now.

00:17:18.088 --> 00:17:20.630
So what we have here is this
the Packet environment I set up.

00:17:20.630 --> 00:17:22.540
So in Packet, all I did was--

00:17:22.540 --> 00:17:24.319
Obviously, this is
a demo, so I didn't

00:17:24.319 --> 00:17:25.527
want to spend too much money.

00:17:25.527 --> 00:17:28.220
So I just spun up
one big machine

00:17:28.220 --> 00:17:30.170
that has a lot of availability.

00:17:30.170 --> 00:17:34.010
So it's got you 64
gigs of RAM, some SSD

00:17:34.010 --> 00:17:36.890
is on there, a
24-core processor.

00:17:36.890 --> 00:17:40.400
So it's sizable, good enough
to let me run my environment.

00:17:40.400 --> 00:17:42.850
What's on there, though, the
most important thing to start

00:17:42.850 --> 00:17:45.930
is I've actually got
VMware running on there.

00:17:45.930 --> 00:17:49.370
And so I've got an
environment there

00:17:49.370 --> 00:17:53.920
setup, which actually manages
all the VMware infrastructure.

00:17:53.920 --> 00:17:56.100
So you can see all the
virtual machines in here.

00:17:56.100 --> 00:17:58.260
Just like that
picture before, you've

00:17:58.260 --> 00:18:00.810
got you know admin workstation.

00:18:00.810 --> 00:18:03.180
You've got the F5
service appliance.

00:18:03.180 --> 00:18:07.350
You've got master nodes,
the admin master nodes.

00:18:07.350 --> 00:18:09.240
You've got a
networkservicesvm that

00:18:09.240 --> 00:18:11.250
handles sort of
NATting and routing

00:18:11.250 --> 00:18:12.360
between that environment.

00:18:12.360 --> 00:18:15.360
Now, this is a specific
configuration for this demo.

00:18:15.360 --> 00:18:17.105
It doesn't have to
run exactly like that.

00:18:17.105 --> 00:18:19.230
And then there's the user
cluster component sitting

00:18:19.230 --> 00:18:21.100
there and a vCenter Appliance.

00:18:21.100 --> 00:18:23.130
So that's what we have
sitting in there in terms

00:18:23.130 --> 00:18:24.610
of that infrastructure.

00:18:24.610 --> 00:18:26.880
So what that gives
me is the ability

00:18:26.880 --> 00:18:36.370
to go over here and say, kubectl
get pods --all-namespaces.

00:18:39.630 --> 00:18:43.153
So you can see here I now have
all these individual things

00:18:43.153 --> 00:18:44.070
running automatically.

00:18:44.070 --> 00:18:46.290
I've got the gke-connect-agent
already in there.

00:18:46.290 --> 00:18:46.990
That's great.

00:18:46.990 --> 00:18:49.590
I've got Istio, is already
pre-installed in there.

00:18:49.590 --> 00:18:53.372
We use Istio for some Layer 7
routing inside of GKE On-Prem.

00:18:53.372 --> 00:18:55.830
You can obviously have a full
Istio deployment as well with

00:18:55.830 --> 00:18:57.630
all the components.

00:18:57.630 --> 00:19:00.110
Alert managers,
calico is in there.

00:19:00.110 --> 00:19:01.890
You see configuration
management.

00:19:01.890 --> 00:19:03.630
We'll talk about
that in a little bit.

00:19:03.630 --> 00:19:08.190
kube proxies, there's some
F5 load balancer mapping.

00:19:08.190 --> 00:19:09.340
Prometheus is in there.

00:19:09.340 --> 00:19:12.460
So you've got logging
built-in baked in the cluster.

00:19:12.460 --> 00:19:13.890
Now, the other
thing that happens,

00:19:13.890 --> 00:19:16.015
of course, as I mentioned,
is that you can actually

00:19:16.015 --> 00:19:18.485
see this cluster
directly in the console.

00:19:18.485 --> 00:19:20.610
I'll reconnect that guy,
and we'll come back to him

00:19:20.610 --> 00:19:22.730
in a second.

00:19:22.730 --> 00:19:25.180
So if I go back here
to Kubernetes Engine,

00:19:25.180 --> 00:19:32.710
I can see in my clusters that
there's my user-cluster1,

00:19:32.710 --> 00:19:35.320
and my location is sjc.

00:19:35.320 --> 00:19:41.785
So that's not actually
a GKE [CHUCKLES] domain.

00:19:41.785 --> 00:19:45.070
And so I can jump in
here, and you can actually

00:19:45.070 --> 00:19:47.510
see-- even on the storage
information right here,

00:19:47.510 --> 00:19:52.930
look, you can see this is
datastore1 kubevols, vmdk.

00:19:52.930 --> 00:19:55.870
So it's mapping to the
actual storage that's

00:19:55.870 --> 00:19:58.540
provided underneath the covers
as part of a VMware vCenter

00:19:58.540 --> 00:19:59.710
setup.

00:19:59.710 --> 00:20:01.740
So you've got the
storage in there.

00:20:01.740 --> 00:20:04.240
And then, of course, you even
the nodes information in here.

00:20:07.030 --> 00:20:10.450
So you have each of the nodes,
what's available, 4 CPUs.

00:20:10.450 --> 00:20:13.930
Out of 8 gigs, only 2.6 is used.

00:20:13.930 --> 00:20:16.540
And I can dive into
an individual node

00:20:16.540 --> 00:20:18.373
and see the actual
detail of what's running.

00:20:18.373 --> 00:20:20.165
Here's all the components
that are running,

00:20:20.165 --> 00:20:21.370
here's the CPUs available.

00:20:21.370 --> 00:20:22.828
I can even see my
conditions about,

00:20:22.828 --> 00:20:24.820
is it fail, like
any checks on disk

00:20:24.820 --> 00:20:28.780
pressure or insufficient memory,
things like that-- all visible

00:20:28.780 --> 00:20:30.700
right there in the console.

00:20:30.700 --> 00:20:34.690
I can also go ahead, and
if I'm really daring--

00:20:34.690 --> 00:20:36.727
because well, hey,
why not, I'm here;

00:20:36.727 --> 00:20:38.810
the internet seems to be
working, this is awesome;

00:20:38.810 --> 00:20:39.910
let's do it--

00:20:39.910 --> 00:20:43.390
I can also just deploy
stuff directly from here

00:20:43.390 --> 00:20:44.210
into that cluster.

00:20:44.210 --> 00:20:45.585
So let's just go
ahead and deploy

00:20:45.585 --> 00:20:47.900
nginx, because that's
fairly straightforward.

00:20:47.900 --> 00:20:51.577
And we'll call it
nginx-1, that makes sense.

00:20:51.577 --> 00:20:52.660
And there's user-cluster1.

00:20:52.660 --> 00:20:53.765
See, it's in the list.

00:20:53.765 --> 00:20:55.390
There's user-cluster1,
which is the one

00:20:55.390 --> 00:20:57.670
that I have running On-Prem.

00:20:57.670 --> 00:21:01.980
And I can go ahead and
Deploy right there.

00:21:01.980 --> 00:21:04.450
And then we watch, and
stare, and cross our fingers,

00:21:04.450 --> 00:21:06.370
and tap the table,
and cross our toes.

00:21:06.370 --> 00:21:10.850
And oh, first green check
mark, second green check mark.

00:21:10.850 --> 00:21:13.250
And I'm going to go for
that it's going to happen,

00:21:13.250 --> 00:21:14.125
it's going to happen.

00:21:18.170 --> 00:21:19.795
Waiting for Pods,
Waiting for Pods.

00:21:22.500 --> 00:21:23.480
All right.

00:21:23.480 --> 00:21:25.520
So that is deployed.

00:21:25.520 --> 00:21:30.690
So now, if I were a GKE, I
could be like, oh, that's sweet.

00:21:30.690 --> 00:21:31.700
So here's nginx-1.

00:21:31.700 --> 00:21:32.325
That's awesome.

00:21:32.325 --> 00:21:34.825
I'm just going to tap in here
and go, cool, let's Expose it,

00:21:34.825 --> 00:21:36.710
because that's what I
would do on GKE, right?

00:21:36.710 --> 00:21:38.750
Just hit Expose.

00:21:38.750 --> 00:21:40.160
There's only one
challenge there,

00:21:40.160 --> 00:21:43.820
which is that if
you view this YAML,

00:21:43.820 --> 00:21:48.068
