00:23:29.650 --> 00:23:30.364
[APPLAUSE]

00:23:30.364 --> 00:23:33.256
[CHUCKLES]

00:23:37.400 --> 00:23:38.900
Let's go back to
the presentation.

00:23:38.900 --> 00:23:44.820
[LAUGHS] But of course,
the challenge here

00:23:44.820 --> 00:23:46.800
is managing multicloud
hybrid environments

00:23:46.800 --> 00:23:49.110
is super difficult,
because you've

00:23:49.110 --> 00:23:51.360
got all sorts of central
IT and governments

00:23:51.360 --> 00:23:52.560
rules people want to apply.

00:23:52.560 --> 00:23:54.940
They want to make sure
it's consistent everywhere.

00:23:54.940 --> 00:23:57.170
And this is just
creating sprawl, right?

00:23:57.170 --> 00:23:59.760
Now, a single person, a
single node or cluster

00:23:59.760 --> 00:24:01.260
has multiple
containers and layers

00:24:01.260 --> 00:24:03.420
of layers of containers
and microservices.

00:24:03.420 --> 00:24:05.320
It adds a lot of complexity.

00:24:05.320 --> 00:24:07.680
So what they really
want in central

00:24:07.680 --> 00:24:09.018
IT is this change control.

00:24:09.018 --> 00:24:10.060
They want accountability.

00:24:10.060 --> 00:24:13.080
They want a consistent
environment configurations.

00:24:13.080 --> 00:24:15.663
They want to ensure consistency
with a single source of truth.

00:24:15.663 --> 00:24:18.038
And so they want to be able
to remediate problems quickly

00:24:18.038 --> 00:24:20.490
should something happen, even
across multiple environments

00:24:20.490 --> 00:24:21.580
around the world.

00:24:21.580 --> 00:24:24.563
So by adding this flexibility,
we're also adding complexity.

00:24:24.563 --> 00:24:25.980
So how are we going
to solve that?

00:24:25.980 --> 00:24:28.000
That's the real question.

00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:31.807
So that's where Anthos
Config Management comes in.

00:24:31.807 --> 00:24:33.390
Anthos Config
Management, you probably

00:24:33.390 --> 00:24:34.785
saw this in the keynote.

00:24:34.785 --> 00:24:36.618
I don't know if anyone
was paying attention.

00:24:36.618 --> 00:24:38.400
There was at one
point the keynote

00:24:38.400 --> 00:24:42.660
where they said git push on
one of the console screens.

00:24:42.660 --> 00:24:43.550
Anyone catch that?

00:24:43.550 --> 00:24:44.830
Anyone see the git push?

00:24:44.830 --> 00:24:45.330
Yeah.

00:24:45.330 --> 00:24:47.490
So that git push is this.

00:24:47.490 --> 00:24:48.510
It's config management.

00:24:48.510 --> 00:24:50.770
It's basically saying,
look, let's just use

00:24:50.770 --> 00:24:54.180
Git as a single source of truth,
and let's make the clusters all

00:24:54.180 --> 00:24:57.420
watch a repository and then
grab the configuration they

00:24:57.420 --> 00:24:59.235
need for their setup.

00:24:59.235 --> 00:25:01.110
So the idea here is you
can sync your configs

00:25:01.110 --> 00:25:05.080
across all your clusters,
both on-prem and in the cloud.

00:25:05.080 --> 00:25:06.990
And this enables
continuous configuration

00:25:06.990 --> 00:25:08.290
of enforcement policy.

00:25:08.290 --> 00:25:12.420
So you can say, for these
namespaces, for these clusters,

00:25:12.420 --> 00:25:14.870
these are the YAML
files you have to apply.

00:25:14.870 --> 00:25:15.495
It's in a repo.

00:25:15.495 --> 00:25:16.890
It automatically happens.

00:25:16.890 --> 00:25:19.420
And so if things change, it'll
go back and look at that repo

00:25:19.420 --> 00:25:21.170
and be like, oh,
something changed.

00:25:21.170 --> 00:25:22.440
I got to update the cluster.

00:25:22.440 --> 00:25:24.660
If you try and make
changes to the cluster that

00:25:24.660 --> 00:25:26.850
disagree with the
repo, it will get them

00:25:26.850 --> 00:25:28.408
back to what the repo says.

00:25:28.408 --> 00:25:30.950
So it actually will make sure
that environment stays in sync.

00:25:30.950 --> 00:25:32.450
And we'll see that
in just a minute.

00:25:32.450 --> 00:25:33.480
I have more demos.

00:25:33.480 --> 00:25:34.227
Yes, I know.

00:25:34.227 --> 00:25:35.560
Get comfortable in those chairs.

00:25:35.560 --> 00:25:38.700
Don't fall off.

00:25:38.700 --> 00:25:41.810
And this gives you auditability
through policy-as-code.

00:25:41.810 --> 00:25:45.840
So now, you can also apply your
own checks, your own controls,

00:25:45.840 --> 00:25:47.940
your own pipelines on
top of that repo that

00:25:47.940 --> 00:25:49.830
allow you to make sure the
right people are doing it,

00:25:49.830 --> 00:25:51.480
they're going through the
right checks and policies.

00:25:51.480 --> 00:25:52.950
And there's even
a tool associated

00:25:52.950 --> 00:25:55.680
with this that will vet your
configuration before you deploy

00:25:55.680 --> 00:25:58.140
it, so it makes sure
it actually will work,

00:25:58.140 --> 00:26:00.520
so there's components in there.

00:26:00.520 --> 00:26:02.730
And so the idea here, though,
is it's really adopting

00:26:02.730 --> 00:26:04.640
that config-as-code model.

00:26:04.640 --> 00:26:06.480
It allows an Anthos
admin, somebody

00:26:06.480 --> 00:26:08.640
who has accesses to
this policy repository,

00:26:08.640 --> 00:26:11.970
to make sure they set up
the environment consistently

00:26:11.970 --> 00:26:14.200
across the board.

00:26:14.200 --> 00:26:16.190
And so this is
kind of an example.

00:26:16.190 --> 00:26:18.930
As part of the docs this rolls
out, you'll see this example.

00:26:18.930 --> 00:26:21.000
This is a policy repo.

00:26:21.000 --> 00:26:22.920
And you can see
there's foo-corp being

00:26:22.920 --> 00:26:25.260
sort of a top-level
namespace with online

00:26:25.260 --> 00:26:27.540
being a namespace,
shipping-app-backend

00:26:27.540 --> 00:26:28.848
being a namespace.

00:26:28.848 --> 00:26:31.140
Now, the online is more
virtual, because there's really

00:26:31.140 --> 00:26:31.890
nothing inside of it.

00:26:31.890 --> 00:26:33.640
But as you get down
to, say, shipping-dev,

00:26:33.640 --> 00:26:36.420
you see there's a
job-creator-rolebinding.yaml,

00:26:36.420 --> 00:26:38.668
job-creator-role.yaml,
namespace.yaml, quota.yaml.

00:26:38.668 --> 00:26:40.710
You can have a whole bunch
of YAML files in here.

00:26:40.710 --> 00:26:44.160
And basically, anything you can
do to a cluster with a kubectl

00:26:44.160 --> 00:26:48.930
apply yaml type command is
something you could mock up

00:26:48.930 --> 00:26:50.250
as config as well.

00:26:50.250 --> 00:26:52.140
So you can actually
do rolebindings,

00:26:52.140 --> 00:26:54.060
you can do
deployments, you can do

00:26:54.060 --> 00:26:57.420
service deployments, workloads,
whatever makes sense.

00:26:57.420 --> 00:26:59.490
And so the idea here
is that you can then

00:26:59.490 --> 00:27:01.810
use your classic
configuration formats,

00:27:01.810 --> 00:27:04.920
YAML files, and then go
ahead and put those into Git

00:27:04.920 --> 00:27:06.420
as a repo.

00:27:06.420 --> 00:27:09.870
And then it plugs it easily
into your CI/CD pipeline

00:27:09.870 --> 00:27:14.530
for making sure that it's
valid and correct as you go.

00:27:14.530 --> 00:27:19.177
So the way this kind of works
is you might have-- in your GKE

00:27:19.177 --> 00:27:21.760
platform, you're going to have
the config management operator.

00:27:21.760 --> 00:27:24.570
So this is implemented
as a Kubernetes operator,

00:27:24.570 --> 00:27:28.230
which basically is then keeping
track of a Git-compliant repo.

00:27:28.230 --> 00:27:30.240
Your repo could be on-prem.

00:27:30.240 --> 00:27:33.180
And your GKE On-Prem has
the operator installed.

00:27:33.180 --> 00:27:37.800
It's syncing against that repo,
just like the GKE is as well.

00:27:37.800 --> 00:27:41.280
So if your repo is
accessible to the GKE,

00:27:41.280 --> 00:27:42.510
then you can use one repo.

00:27:42.510 --> 00:27:46.980
You could also use, say,
GitHub or a private Git

00:27:46.980 --> 00:27:49.380
repo if you want
deployed in the cloud

00:27:49.380 --> 00:27:51.430
and then use that as
your point as well.

00:27:51.430 --> 00:27:55.432
Also, you can use Google
Source Repositories.

00:27:55.432 --> 00:27:56.640
So let's take a look at that.

