00:35:41.570 --> 00:35:42.950
And what Matt has
shown is really

00:35:42.950 --> 00:35:46.310
what's going to help us to have
that sort of single control

00:35:46.310 --> 00:35:50.170
plane and still be able to
use both on-prem and GKE.

00:35:53.670 --> 00:35:56.650
Just some numbers around
what I call our complexity,

00:35:56.650 --> 00:35:59.730
so I said 200 customers.

00:35:59.730 --> 00:36:02.550
We build what we
call a CasinoModule

00:36:02.550 --> 00:36:04.320
for each and every customer.

00:36:04.320 --> 00:36:07.190
So each customer has at
least one CasinoModule.

00:36:07.190 --> 00:36:10.710
So we have 500 CasinoModules,
translating into something

00:36:10.710 --> 00:36:14.250
around 3,500 VMs.

00:36:14.250 --> 00:36:16.020
So it's quite a lot
to keep track of.

00:36:16.020 --> 00:36:18.690
We have 10 data centers
around the world

00:36:18.690 --> 00:36:22.380
to support this and support
the licenses we have.

00:36:22.380 --> 00:36:26.310
And as I said, 200
games, adding new games,

00:36:26.310 --> 00:36:31.290
adding new customers, it's kind
of like the complexity is just

00:36:31.290 --> 00:36:33.810
increasing a lot.

00:36:33.810 --> 00:36:39.090
So adding that hybrid without
having really control over it

00:36:39.090 --> 00:36:40.370
would be really hard for us.

00:36:43.510 --> 00:36:46.070
These are current data centers.

00:36:46.070 --> 00:36:47.005
And we're growing.

00:36:49.780 --> 00:36:55.090
We are setting one up
right now in Pennsylvania

00:36:55.090 --> 00:36:58.130
for the opening of that market.

00:36:58.130 --> 00:37:01.340
The beauty of how we see
GKE On-Prem falling out

00:37:01.340 --> 00:37:04.730
is that we can set up our data
centers in a different way

00:37:04.730 --> 00:37:06.540
than we've done before.

00:37:06.540 --> 00:37:10.130
So we've been working
with container platforms

00:37:10.130 --> 00:37:12.950
and in Kubernetes for
the last two years.

00:37:12.950 --> 00:37:20.260
And we run OpenShift clusters
in all our 10 data center.

00:37:20.260 --> 00:37:22.598
And it's not a
trivial thing to do.

00:37:22.598 --> 00:37:24.390
It's quite a lot of
maintenance to do this.

00:37:24.390 --> 00:37:25.800
And we were looking
for a managed

00:37:25.800 --> 00:37:27.920
service for quite some time.

00:37:27.920 --> 00:37:29.140
And now we got it, right?

00:37:31.670 --> 00:37:35.060
So this is pretty much
the sort of migration path

00:37:35.060 --> 00:37:36.710
